{
  "action": "send",
  "subject": "HN 50-200: price, cash flow and effective date",
  "body": "Dear Thomas,\n\nI understand that higher iron and wage costs are putting pressure on your foundry. We want a workable annual agreement, not another one-sided squeeze.\n\nBecause energy has moved differently, [[c3]] which is why we ask for a smaller overall adjustment rather than the full 9.5%.\n\nOn timing, [[c7]] which supports January implementation; an earlier date would need a compensating price benefit.\n\nWe also acknowledge our late payments. If you can offer the corresponding price, we can commit to punctual payment under one of these packages, each for 8,150 pieces over 12 months:\n\nA: EUR 124.98, 1 November, 14 days net.\nB: EUR 126.32, 1 January, 30 days net.\nC: EUR 128.14, 1 April, 60 days net.\n\nAll retain fixed pricing, FCA and 42-day lead time.\n\nWhich package gives you the strongest basis to improve your price?\n\nHollerau Pumpenwerke, Purchasing",
  "term_sheets": [
    {
      "unit_price": 124.98,
      "effective_date": "2026-11-01",
      "payment_days": 14
    },
    {
      "unit_price": 126.32,
      "effective_date": "2027-01-01",
      "payment_days": 30
    },
    {
      "unit_price": 128.14,
      "effective_date": "2027-04-01",
      "payment_days": 60
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Thomas stresses input-cost pressure, while the correspondence shows that faster cash matters to him. These three credible anchors test early payment against implementation timing and longer payment, supported by the energy movement and contractual notice requirement.",
  "target_price": 128.85
}

